1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,100
Here's the cover we made last time.

2
00:00:02,150 --> 00:00:06,412
A recipe book needs recipes, so
in this episode we'll add pages,

3
00:00:06,412 --> 00:00:11,723
give them a design of their own, and
have each page fill in its own details.

4
00:00:11,933 --> 00:00:13,903
Two ideas carry the whole app.

5
00:00:14,020 --> 00:00:16,720
The first is the hierarchy, on the left.

6
00:00:16,858 --> 00:00:20,077
Every page in your document
is a node in this tree,

7
00:00:20,077 --> 00:00:23,294
and right now there's
just one: Family Recipes.

8
00:00:23,500 --> 00:00:25,520
The second idea is templates.

9
00:00:25,586 --> 00:00:27,976
A node doesn't hold a design itself.

10
00:00:28,062 --> 00:00:31,652
It points at a template, which
holds the shapes and the text.

11
00:00:31,779 --> 00:00:34,499
This cover points at one called A4 Blank.

12
00:00:34,704 --> 00:00:38,965
To add a page inside the cover,
hover its row and click the plus.

13
00:00:39,109 --> 00:00:41,649
There is the new page, tucked underneath.

14
00:00:41,797 --> 00:00:44,777
Select it, and it looks
exactly like the cover.

15
00:00:45,069 --> 00:00:49,085
That's because a new page borrows
the first template in the project,

16
00:00:49,085 --> 00:00:50,989
and so far there's only one.

17
00:00:51,125 --> 00:00:54,425
I'll give it a name before
we fix that: Tomato Soup.

18
00:00:54,634 --> 00:00:57,104
Templates live behind
this switch at the top.

19
00:00:57,233 --> 00:01:01,014
New Template adds an empty one,
and the pencil renames it.

20
00:01:01,150 --> 00:01:02,730
I'll call it Recipe.

21
00:01:02,938 --> 00:01:04,518
One thing to watch for.

22
00:01:04,665 --> 00:01:09,343
New templates start at the size of
a reMarkable Paper Pro, not A4.

23
00:01:09,476 --> 00:01:14,006
Page Presets, in Template Settings,
makes it A4 to match the cover.

24
00:01:14,212 --> 00:01:16,812
I'd like the same dotted
card as the cover.

25
00:01:16,959 --> 00:01:21,719
So I'll open the cover's template, click
the card, and copy it with Control C.

26
00:01:21,927 --> 00:01:24,887
Back to Recipe, and paste with Control V.

27
00:01:24,954 --> 00:01:29,208
Pasted things always land twenty
points down and to the right,

28
00:01:29,208 --> 00:01:33,644
even on a different template,
so I'll set X and Y back to 24.

29
00:01:37,293 --> 00:01:39,813
Now the template needs a page to use it.

30
00:01:39,904 --> 00:01:45,524
Back in the hierarchy, select Tomato Soup,
and set its Assigned Template to Recipe.

31
00:01:45,650 --> 00:01:47,650
The cover keeps its own design.

32
00:01:49,311 --> 00:01:52,071
Every recipe needs its name at the top.

33
00:01:52,160 --> 00:01:56,976
But if I type Tomato Soup into the template,
every recipe will be called Tomato

34
00:01:56,976 --> 00:01:57,530
Soup.

35
00:01:57,678 --> 00:02:00,238
What the template needs is a placeholder.

36
00:02:00,445 --> 00:02:05,390
Drag out a text box, and type the word
title inside double curly braces.

37
00:02:05,476 --> 00:02:10,416
Press Escape, and the placeholder turns
into the name of the page: Tomato Soup.

38
00:02:10,623 --> 00:02:12,423
And which page is it showing?

39
00:02:12,554 --> 00:02:14,744
The one picked in Preview, up here.

40
00:02:14,806 --> 00:02:19,003
While you design, it decides whose
details fill the placeholders.

41
00:02:19,119 --> 00:02:22,629
And title works everywhere,
because every page has one.

42
00:02:22,837 --> 00:02:26,060
Now to style it like the
cover: Playfair Display,

43
00:02:26,060 --> 00:02:29,007
34 points, bold, and the same deep brown.

44
00:02:29,107 --> 00:02:33,497
The styling lives on the template
too, so every recipe page gets it.

45
00:02:34,484 --> 00:02:36,364
On to the other recipes.

46
00:02:36,414 --> 00:02:41,294
In the hierarchy, Duplicate, on the row,
copies a page along with its template.

47
00:02:41,347 --> 00:02:44,537
The copy arrives with Copy
on the end of its name,

48
00:02:44,537 --> 00:02:49,957
so I'll rename it Banana Bread, then
duplicate that one for a Weeknight Pad Thai.

49
00:02:55,054 --> 00:02:57,944
Click through them, and each
one shows its own name.

50
00:02:57,999 --> 00:03:02,153
One template, three pages, and nothing
on the template had to change.

51
00:03:02,359 --> 00:03:06,163
A recipe card wants more than a
name, though: how many it serves,

52
00:03:06,163 --> 00:03:07,700
and how long it takes.

53
00:03:07,741 --> 00:03:09,591
Those go in data fields.

54
00:03:09,664 --> 00:03:13,464
Select Tomato Soup, click Add,
and call the field servings.

55
00:03:13,834 --> 00:03:15,614
Give it a value: 4.

56
00:03:15,665 --> 00:03:19,330
And a second field,
time, set to 35 minutes.

57
00:03:19,398 --> 00:03:23,275
Values are plain text, so
write them however reads best.

58
00:03:23,482 --> 00:03:27,283
Over in the template, I'll add
a second line, under the title:

59
00:03:27,283 --> 00:03:30,230
a text box that starts
with the word Serves.

60
00:03:30,265 --> 00:03:34,935
It picked up the title's style, so
I'll make it 16 points, and not bold.

61
00:03:35,676 --> 00:03:36,976
Now the fields.

62
00:03:37,119 --> 00:03:40,660
The buttons under Insert Data
Field add placeholders for you,

63
00:03:40,660 --> 00:03:42,709
so there's nothing to misspell.

64
00:03:42,765 --> 00:03:45,135
Servings, a separator, and time.

65
00:03:45,354 --> 00:03:46,614
Here's the catch.

66
00:03:46,687 --> 00:03:50,692
Switch the preview to Banana Bread,
and the line just says Serves.

67
00:03:50,744 --> 00:03:55,074
Banana Bread has no fields yet, and
a missing field comes out blank.

68
00:03:55,157 --> 00:03:57,077
No error, and no warning.

69
00:03:57,182 --> 00:04:00,102
So it pays to flip through
the preview now and then.

70
00:04:00,311 --> 00:04:04,048
Fields belong to each page,
so Banana Bread gets its own:

71
00:04:04,048 --> 00:04:06,920
servings 8, and an hour and ten minutes.

72
00:04:07,015 --> 00:04:10,575
Field names have to match
exactly, capitals included.

73
00:04:10,623 --> 00:04:13,833
Servings with a capital S
would be a different field.

74
00:04:14,041 --> 00:04:19,115
And the same two fields for the
Pad Thai: serves 2, in 25 minutes.

75
00:04:19,231 --> 00:04:22,831
For three recipes, typing
fields in by hand is fine.

76
00:04:22,879 --> 00:04:28,719
For dozens, the generator in episode 10
builds the pages and their fields from code.

77
00:04:28,926 --> 00:04:31,506
One more idea, and it's a handy one.

78
00:04:31,572 --> 00:04:34,372
I'd like each card to
say whose recipe it is.

79
00:04:34,405 --> 00:04:37,139
So the template gets a
small line at the bottom:

80
00:04:37,139 --> 00:04:40,065
From the kitchen of, and
an author placeholder.

81
00:04:41,761 --> 00:04:44,141
No page has an author, so it's blank.

82
00:04:44,221 --> 00:04:47,890
Rather than adding one to every
recipe, I'll add it once,

83
00:04:47,890 --> 00:04:50,393
to the cover: author, Grandma Rose.

84
00:04:50,614 --> 00:04:52,564
And every recipe picks it up.

85
00:04:52,703 --> 00:04:55,995
When a page doesn't have a
field, the app asks its parent,

86
00:04:55,995 --> 00:04:59,083
then the parent's parent,
all the way up the tree.

87
00:04:59,289 --> 00:05:01,499
A page's own value still wins.

88
00:05:01,620 --> 00:05:06,216
The Pad Thai is Dad's recipe, so it
gets an author field of its own,

89
00:05:06,216 --> 00:05:08,291
and only that page changes.

90
00:05:08,499 --> 00:05:10,439
Let's export it and flip through.

91
00:05:10,489 --> 00:05:14,619
Export PDF builds the whole
document right in your browser.

92
00:05:14,909 --> 00:05:19,307
The cover, then three recipes, each
one filled in from its own fields,

93
00:05:19,307 --> 00:05:22,949
with Grandma Rose on two of
them and Dad on the third.

94
00:05:23,087 --> 00:05:27,567
The rest of each page stays empty,
for writing the recipe out by hand.

95
00:05:27,772 --> 00:05:32,344
Next time, the cover gets an index:
a data grid that lists every recipe,

96
00:05:32,344 --> 00:05:33,932
and links straight to it.
